# Free functions and module-level bindings have no generated surface

**DONE.** Closed before these files carried a status word. The `.done`
suffix is the record; this line is that same answer in the second place
the check reads, and no new judgement.

Found in the 2026-08-25 review.

## Problem

The stated goal is complete surface coverage generated from the
binding specifications. Free functions are outside it entirely.

pxd.py appears to parse `describe_store` into api["free_functions"],
and generate.py reads only api["classes"]. So the parse result looked
like dead data.

CORRECTION (2026-08-25): it was never parsed at all. A free function
inside `cdef extern from ...:` arrives as a CVarDefNode carrying a
CFuncDeclaratorNode - the same shape a class method has, one level up.
extract_api looked for a CFuncDefNode, which never matched, so the
list was silently always empty. Two bugs stacked: nothing produced the
data, and nothing consumed it, so neither was visible.

Three module-level bindings have no async wrapper, no manifest entry
and no RPC surface: `describe(obj)` (the C++ virtual-dispatch
trampoline demo), `gc_stats()` and `collect_garbage()`. A remote
client cannot ask the server for collector counters or trigger a
collection - the two things a long-lived evaluation server (016) most
obviously needs.

## Why it matters

Real libstore and libexpr are full of free functions: openStore,
parseStorePath, computeFSClosure. If free functions stay outside the
generator, a large part of the real surface is hand-written forever,
which is exactly what this design exists to avoid.

## Direction

- A module-level function needs the same policy markers a class
  carries. Function attributes work in Python
  (`collect_garbage._threading = "pool"`) but read badly. Consider a
  module-level registry in the pyx, or a decorator the codegen reads.
- collect_garbage is process-global and blocking, not a method on
  anything. Its threading policy is "pool", and the async wrapper
  should be a module-level coroutine, not a class.
- gRPC has no free functions: they need a service to live on. A
  per-module service (`huggorm_bindingsService`) is the obvious shape.
- describe(obj) takes a proxy argument and returns a scalar. The
  existing WireCodec handles that unchanged.

Blocks nothing today; 016 will want gc_stats over the wire.

## Done 2026-08-25, except for two named blockers

Module-level functions opt in by declaring `_threading`, the same
marker a class carries - and the marker is what opts them in, so a
helper the module happens to export stays out of the surface. "pool"
is the only policy allowed: a free function has no instance and
therefore no thread to be affine to. Anything else is rejected by
name.

They introspect FAR better than cdef classes - real signatures, real
annotations, real docstrings, a settable __dict__ - so the live
function supplies nearly everything and the pxd fills only what Cython
drops. `describe(obj)` is untyped in the pyx and `const CStore&` in
the pxd, so the emitted wrapper says `obj: Store`.

The pyx name and the pxd name differ (describe vs describe_store), so
free functions carry `_binds` too, exactly like classes (020).
Claiming a name the pxd does not declare fails the build.

Emitted as module-level coroutines in free_functions.py, hopping
through the shared pool via a new runtime helper. The hand-written
`asyncio.to_thread(huggorm_bindings.collect_garbage)` in the smoke test and
the demo is gone.

RPC: one shared FunctionsService, since a free function has no
instance to hang off. Requests carry no `self` handle - the only
structural difference from a method. Everything else is the existing
codec.

## The two blockers, now named by the build

Only collect_garbage crosses the wire today. The generator prints why
the others cannot, and the manifest records it per function in
`wire_blockers`, so the client refuses them with the reason rather
than failing obscurely:

    warning: describe has no RPC surface - parameter 'obj': Store is
      not in the manifest, so it has no wire policy (an excluded base
      class, most likely - see tasks/018)
    warning: gc_stats has no RPC surface - return type: dict has no
      wire representation; the schema has no map or struct type yet

So describe is blocked on 018 - Store is abstract and excluded from
generation, so there is no name for "any store" on the wire, nor for
"any async store" in the in-process annotation either. That is the
same missing base 018 exists to add, arrived at from a second
direction.

gc_stats needs a map or struct wire type. Worth doing when 016 wants
collector counters remotely; not before.
